From 53a7cf752086444ee1f0a9f2296ab665f814eedf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Milan Soni <milansoni96946@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 12:32:36 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(routing): normalize time remaining in reset-window
 strategy (#9330)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The reset-window strategy could rank an account that resets in 26 days
ahead of accounts resetting within the week.

`getResetWindowTimestampMs` only recognised a reset instant when the quota
snapshot exposed a canonically named window — the structural `window5h` /
`window7d` / `windowWeekly` / `windowMonthly` fields, or a `windows` map
keyed by "weekly" | "session" | "monthly". Providers routed through
`genericQuotaFetcher.convertUsageToQuotaInfo` (Antigravity, and every other
provider backed by `getUsageForProvider`) key their `windows` map by MODEL
ID instead, so nothing matched and the helper fell through to the
single-signal `quota.resetAt` — which that converter populates from the
most-USED window and leaves null entirely while every window is still at 0%
used. Those accounts scored `Infinity` ("never resets") and sorted BEHIND a
Codex account whose secondary window carried a parseable 26-day reset.

Two narrower defects rode along: a window that was structurally present but
carried `resetAt: null` short-circuited (`a || b`) past a sibling that did
know when it reset, and ordering compared raw epoch timestamps, so a
snapshot stale by days jumped the queue over one that had just reset.

- resolve the configured window to the first candidate that actually carries
  a reset instant, instead of the first that merely exists
- fall back to the earliest reset across every `windows` entry, whatever the
  provider named them, before the single-signal `quota.resetAt` (reads
  `windows` only — Codex narrows it to the Spark scope on purpose, so
  `allWindows` stays out)
- sort on `getResetWindowRemainingMs`: milliseconds until reset against one
  `now` snapshot for the whole ranking, clamped at 0, `Infinity` last

Exhausted (`limitReached`) accounts still resolve to `Infinity` and stay
demoted, and canonically named windows keep their existing resolution.

Regression guard: tests/unit/combo/reset-window-strategy-9330.test.ts —
8 cases, including the reported layout end-to-end through
`orderTargetsByResetWindow` (Codex first in the combo definition, the
5-day Antigravity account must be dispatched first).
